abel":"Denis Sassou-Nguesso","description":"President of the Republic of the Congo (1979–1992; 1997–present)","enwiki":"Denis Sassou Nguesso","summary":"Denis Sassou Nguesso (French pronunciation: [dəni sasu ŋɡeso]; born 23 November 1943) is a Congolese politician and former military officer who has served as president of the Republic of the Congo since 1997. He also previously served as president of the People's Republic of the Congo from 1979 to 1992.\nSassou Nguesso headed the Congolese Party of Labour (PCT) for 12 years during his first period as president. His daughter Edith Lucie married Gabonese president Omar Bongo in 1989.","image":"Denis Sassou Nguesso on April 29, 2026 (cropped).jpg","birth":1943.8931506849315,"death":null},"Q4752077":{"label":"Anatole Collinet Makosso","description":"Republic of the Congo politician","enwiki":"Anatole Collinet Makosso","summary":"Anatole Collinet Makosso (born 11 March 1965) is a Congolese politician who has served as the prime minister of the Republic of the Congo since 2021. A member of the Congolese Party of Labour, he previously served as Minister of Primary and Secondary Education from 2015 to 2021, and as Minister of Youth and Civic Instruction from 2011 to 2016.","image":"Anatole Collinet Makosso 2022 (cropped).jpg","birth":1965.1890410958904,"death":null},"Q248332":{"label":"Alfred Raoul","description":"President of Congo-Brazzaville from 1968 to 1969","enwiki":"Alfred Raoul","summary":"Alfred Raoul (15 December 1938 – 16 July 1999) was President of the Republic of the Congo from 5 September 1968 to 1 January 1969, and the Prime Minister of the Republic of the Congo from August 1968 to December 1969.\nHe was Vice President of the People's Republic of Congo from January 1970 to December 1971. \nHe was married to Émilienne Raoul.","image":"Alfred Raoul.JPG","birth":1938.9534246575342,"death":1999.5369863013698},"Q2871636":{"label":"Augustin Poignet","description":"Congolese politician (1928-2008)","enwiki":"Augustin Poignet","summary":"Augustin Poignet was the politician from Republic of the Congo who served as President of Senate of the Republic of the Congo.","image":null,"birth":1928.3224043715848,"death":2008.483606557377},"Q379725":{"label":"Pascal Lissouba","description":"First President of the Republic of the Congo (1931–2020)","enwiki":"Pascal Lissouba","summary":"Pascal Lissouba (15 November 1931 – 24 August 2020) was a Congolese politician who was the first democratically elected President of the Republic of the Congo and served from 31 August 1992 until 25 October 1997. He was overthrown by his predecessor and current president Denis Sassou Nguesso in the 1997 civil war.","image":"Visit of Pascal Lissouba, President of Congo, to the EC (cropped).jpg","birth":1931.8712328767124,"death":2020.6448087431695},"Q454025":{"label":"Marien Ngouabi","description":"President of Congo-Brazzaville from 1969 to 1977","enwiki":"Marien Ngouabi","summary":"Marien Ngouabi (December 31, 1938 – March 18, 1977) was a Congolese politician and military officer who served as the fourth President of the People's Republic of the Congo from 1969 until his assassination in 1977.","image":"Marien Ngouabi Janvier 1970.png","birth":1938.9972602739726,"death":1977.2082191780821},"Q559442":{"label":"Fulbert Youlou","description":"President of the Republic of the Congo (1917-1972)","enwiki":"Fulbert Youlou","summary":"Fulbert Youlou (19 July 1917 – 6 May 1972) was a Congolese nationalist leader and former Catholic priest who became the first President of the Republic of the Congo upon its independence in 1960.\nIn August 1960, Youlou led his country into independence. In December 1960, he organised an intercontinental conference in Brazzaville, wherein he praised the advantages of economic liberalism and condemned communism. Youlou disappointed many when he imposed a single-party system and imprisoned union leaders in August 1963; this led to the revolution of the Trois Glorieuses (English: \"Three Glorious Days\").","image":"Fulbert Youlou (cropped).JPG","birth":1917.545205479452,"death":1972.344262295082},"Q740411":{"label":"Joachim Yhombi-Opango","description":"Congolese politician (1939–2020)","enwiki":"Joachim Yhombi-Opango","summary":"Jacques Joachim Yhombi-Opango (12 January 1939 – 30 March 2020) was a Congolese politician. He was an army officer who became Congo-Brazzaville's first general and served as Head of State of the People's Republic of the Congo from 1977 to 1979. He was the President of the Rally for Democracy and Development (RDD), a political party, and served as Prime Minister from 1993 to 1996. He was in exile from 1997 to 2007.","image":null,"birth":1939.035616438356,"death":2020.2431693989072},"Q740438":{"label":"Alphonse Massamba-Débat","description":"Congolese politician (1921-1977)","enwiki":"Alphonse Massamba-Débat","summary":"Alphonse Massamba-Débat (February 11, 1921 – March 25, 1977) was a political figure of the Republic of the Congo who led the country from 1963 until 1968 in a one-party system.","image":"Stamp of Congo, Republic (Brazzaville) - 1965 - Colnect 675065 - Président Massamba Debat (cropped).jpeg","birth":1921.1123287671232,"death":1977.227397260274},"Q3169789":{"label":"Jean-Pierre Thystère Tchicaya","description":"Republic of the Congo politician (1936–2008)","enwiki":"Jean-Pierre Thystère Tchicaya","summary":"Jean-Pierre Thystère Tchicaya (January 7, 1936 – June 20, 2008) was a Congolese politician. He was briefly acting head of state of Congo-Brazzaville in February 1979 and was President of the National Assembly from 2002 to 2007. He also led a political party, the Rally for Democracy and Social Progress (RDPS), from 1990 to 2008.","image":null,"birth":1936.016393442623,"death":2008.467213114754}},"media":{"Flag of the Republic of the Congo.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg","author":"See File history below for details.","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of the Republic of the Congo.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Coat_of_arms_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg","author":"Original: Louis Mühlemann Vector:  Fenn-O-maniC","license":"Public domain"},"Denis Sassou Nguesso on April 29, 2026 (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Denis_Sassou_Nguesso_on_April_29%2C_2026_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Denis_Sassou_Nguesso_on_April_29%2C_2026_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denis_Sassou_Nguesso_on_April_29,_2026_(cropped).jpg","author":"Presidential Executive Office","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Anatole Collinet Makosso 2022 (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Anatole_Collinet_Makosso_2022_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Anatole_Collinet_Makosso_2022_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatole_Collinet_Makosso_2022_(cropped).jpg","author":"Christophe Licoppe / European Union, 2022","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Alfred Raoul.JPG":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Alfred_Raoul.JPG/330px-Alfred_Raoul.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_Raoul.JPG","author":"Dibas","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Visit of Pascal Lissouba, President of